      "id": 1,
      "text": "Hugoton was first called Hugo after Victor Hugo, then renamed to avoid confusion with Hugo, Colorado.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugoton%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Hugoton United Methodist Church, founded in 1886, was the first church in Stevens County and still stands today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugoton%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Hugoton became Stevens County’s permanent seat in 1887, though disputes lingered until the 1888 Hay Meadow Massacre.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugoton%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Natural gas found southwest of Hugoton in 1927 sparked the Hugoton Natural Gas Area, turning the town into a gas‑extraction hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugoton%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Santa Fe Railway reached Hugoton in 1913, accelerating growth and connecting the town to the national rail network.",
